PtokaX under systemd
====================

See README.systemd for the unit file itself.

The systemd instance is the hub, named after its HubName setting. A hub with
HubName PXdev runs as ptokax@PXdev with its files under /var/lib/ptokax/PXdev.


Setup
-----

	./configure --prefix=/usr && make -j8
	sudo make install
	sudo systemctl daemon-reload
	sudo ptokaxctl create PXdev
	sudo ptokaxctl setup PXdev
	sudo systemctl enable --now ptokax@PXdev

create seeds cfg/ from cfg.example and assigns ports. The first hub on a host
gets 1209 and 411, later ones free ports from 4111 up.

setup is the configuration menu, the same one as PtokaX -m. Set HubName and
HubAddress there. It refuses to run against a hub that is up.


Paths
-----

	/var/lib/ptokax/HubName/	created by systemd
		cfg/			settings, bans, reglist
		scripts/		.lua files
		texts/			files served by hub commands
		logs/			written only outside a service manager
	/etc/ptokax/HubName.conf	optional, sets PTOKAX_ARGS
	/usr/share/ptokax/language/	16 translations shared by all hubs

A language/ directory inside a hub's own tree overrides the shared one.


Commands
--------

	ptokaxctl list			hubs and state
	ptokaxctl check HubName		configured against listening
	ptokaxctl log HubName -f	journal for one hub
	ptokaxctl backup HubName	tar the hub's tree
	ptokaxctl unit show		installed unit against running systemd
	systemctl reload ptokax@HubName
	systemctl stop ptokax.target	every hub

check output:

	state      /var/lib/ptokax/PXdev
	unit       enabled, active
	ports      1209;411
	listening  Listening on 1209, 411

ports against listening is the comparison worth making. A difference means
something else already had the port. The hub starts on the ports it got and
warns:

	journalctl -u ptokax@PXdev -p warning


Settings
--------

Settings are held in memory and cfg/ is rewritten on shutdown, so an edit made
to cfg/Settings.pxt while the hub is up is overwritten when it stops.

What sticks: an op command in the hub, or ptokaxctl setup with the hub down.

Scripts are not affected. Drop the .lua in scripts/, enable it in
cfg/Scripts.pxt, reload.


Reload and restart
------------------

	reload		log files, MOTD, texts, scripts. Nobody drops.
	restart		also rebinds sockets. Everyone drops.

Ports and settings are picked up on restart only.

A running hub keeps the old binary until it restarts, so an upgrade needs a
restart rather than a reload.


Identity
--------

Each hub runs as ptokax-HubName under DynamicUser=yes and cannot read another
hub's files. cfg/Settings.pxt holds the database password in plaintext, so this
matters when one machine runs hubs for different owners.

	default		transient uid, tree under /var/lib/private at 0700
			root, sudo to look inside
	stable uid	add a static user, see ptokax.sysusers.example
	group access	stable uid plus group-access.conf.example, which drops
			DynamicUser. Tree moves to /var/lib/ptokax/HubName at
			0750, readable and writable by the ptokax-HubName group

Group access trades the DynamicUser hardening for letting more than one person
work on the hub without sudo.

Back up and restore as root. With id-mapped mounts the host shows the files
owned by nobody in the first two modes.


Port 411 on several hubs
------------------------

The kernel binds an address and port together, so it is one 411 per address,
not per host. IPv6 makes addresses cheap.

	ip -6 addr add 2001:db8:1::10/64 dev eth0
	ip -6 addr add 2001:db8:1::11/64 dev eth0

Put them in the network configuration too, the above does not survive a reboot.
Then for each hub, in ptokaxctl setup, set HubAddress to a name that resolves
to its address and turn on bind only single IP.

The bound address is whatever HubAddress resolves to, so this works when the
address you hand out is the address you bind. Behind NAT it is not, and socket
activation covers that case instead, since ListenStream= names the bind address
directly. README.systemd.

Two hubs cannot share one address. The hub sends $Lock before the client says
anything, so a proxy has nothing to route on.

With one IPv4 address and no IPv6, one hub gets 411 and the rest take other
ports. Clients reach those as dchub://example.org:4111.
